(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 9, part: `Part Three: One Chart`, title: `The Build`, weeks: `Months 9 to 12`,
  tasks: [`SD1`,`SD2`,`SD3`,`SC2`,`SC4`,`SC6`,`C4`,`C6`,`E7`,`L22`,`L23`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Tuesday, August 3, Maria came up the stairs at 6:58 with two coffees and the list. The light in the analysts' room was on. It had probably never gone off.`,
    `Kiran was at his desk in yesterday's shirt. The thirty-one failed scripts were on the whiteboard behind him in three colors, sorted by system, then by severity, then by which ones shared a cause. It was beautiful work. It had taken most of the night.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"I went home,"</span> he said before she could ask. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"At one. Then I came back at five, because I knew where to start."</span> He said it like good news.`,
    `She set a coffee in front of him. Monday's page of her legal pad still said KIRAN at the top, underlined twice, the way it had since April. She'd told Kiran to go home so many times it had stopped sounding like anything. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Show me the first one,"</span> she said.`,
    `He put it on the big screen. Script one, the one she'd written herself in April and Walt had voted first. A Rawlins test patient, seventy-four years old, twenty-three active medications, transferred to Greeley. On Corvane's admission screen, twenty medications sat in a neat column. No banner. No count. Nothing to say the list had ever been longer.`,
    `Maria looked at it the way she used to look at a monitor at four in the morning, waiting for a number to change. It didn't. Three medications had left Rawlins and never arrived, and the screen was perfectly calm about it.`,
    `Kiran was already reaching for the keyboard. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"I think I know where. Give me an hour in the build and I can..."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"No,"</span> Maria said, and surprised them both. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Get Marcus. And eat something."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: proving it works`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Six ways to test`,
        body: [`The Sunday after the first failed cycle, Ochoa took the back booth at the Greeley diner and wrote six words down the legal pad, smallest to biggest. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Every one of these answers a different question,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Skip one and you don't skip the question. You just hear it later, from a patient."</span>`,
          `Unit testing checks one piece by itself: an order set, a mapping, one interface's translation. The builder usually runs it. System testing checks a whole application against its design, every screen and rule in Corvane working together. Integrated testing runs real workflows across systems and interfaces, from registration through orders, pharmacy, the eMAR, results, and billing, with test patients. In a migration most defects live between systems, so this is where they turn up.`,
          `Regression testing reruns tests that already passed, after any change, to prove the fix didn't break something else. Volume or load testing runs the expected peak, all of Monday morning at once, to find where the system slows. Stress testing pushes past that peak to find where it breaks. User acceptance testing, UAT, is the last gate. The people who'll use the system run their own scenarios and decide whether it's ready, and its owners sign.`],
        terms: [[`Unit testing`,`Testing one component by itself, such as a single mapping or order set, usually by the person who built it.`],[`Integrated testing`,`Testing workflows across connected systems and interfaces, end to end, to find defects between them.`],[`Regression testing`,`Rerunning tests that passed before, after a change, to confirm nothing that worked is now broken.`],[`User acceptance testing`,`Testing by the people who will use the system, on their own scenarios, to decide whether it's ready.`]],
        exam: `Expect a scenario that describes a test and asks its type. One piece alone is unit, one whole application is system, systems together are integrated, a rerun after a change is regression, peak load is stress or volume, and users deciding readiness is acceptance.` },
      { h: `Count what you find`,
        body: [`Each test script lists steps, test data, and an expected result. When the actual result differs, it's a defect, logged with the steps to reproduce it and a severity: one stops care or risks harm, four is cosmetic. Two measures track each build. Pass rate is scripts passed divided by scripts executed. Defect density is defects divided by the size of what you tested, here per test script. Other teams count per function point or per thousand lines of code. Say which, and use the same one every build.`,
          `Before testing starts, write entry and exit criteria, like 95 percent passed and no open severity one or two defects. Then trace every requirement and every acceptance test in the contract to a script that proves it. That traceability matrix is how you validate a build against the contract and the design specs, and it shows any requirement nobody tested. Test the edges, too. A limit of twenty needs a test at nineteen, twenty, twenty-one, and well past it.`],
        terms: [[`Defect density`,`Defects found divided by the size of what was tested, such as per test script or per function point.`],[`Exit criteria`,`Conditions, set before testing, that a build must meet to finish a test phase or go live.`],[`Traceability matrix`,`A table linking each requirement and contract term to the tests that prove it.`]],
        exam: `Expect to compute a pass rate or defect density from a table. A deliverable is accepted when it passes the test written from the contract and design, not when the vendor says it's done.` },
      { h: `Who touches the build`,
        body: [`Testing needs controls of its own. Keep separate environments for build, test, and production. Give every build a version number, so a passing result points to exactly what passed. Move nothing between environments without a change ticket, and move by ticket, not by date. Separate the duties: whoever builds a change doesn't test it, approve it, or move it alone.`,
          `Test security as well. Sign in with test accounts that carry the same roles real users will have, check the audit logs, and scan for vulnerabilities before go-live. Use test patients or masked data. Real patient data in a test system needs every protection it has in production.`],
        terms: [[`Segregation of duties`,`Splitting key steps, like building, testing, approving, and promoting a change, among different people.`],[`Version control`,`Tracking every change to a build with a version, so tests and releases point to an exact configuration.`]],
        exam: `When one person can build, test, and promote a change, the missing control is segregation of duties. When nobody can say which build passed, it's version control.` },
      { h: `The Thursday board`,
        body: [`Technical change management decides what changes in a live system, and when. A request for change says what will change and why, what it touches, the test evidence, and a back-out plan. The change advisory board weighs the risk, then approves it, schedules it, or sends it back. Standard changes are routine and preapproved. Emergency changes get fast approval from an emergency CAB or a named authority, then a full review after. Before go-live, most teams freeze everything but fixes.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Watch the shared parts,"</span> Ochoa said. When eleven hospitals run on one system, a change built for three of them reaches all eleven, unless somebody proves it doesn't.`],
        terms: [[`Change advisory board`,`The group that reviews requests for change for risk, impact, testing, and timing before they reach a live system.`],[`Back-out plan`,`The tested steps to undo a change and restore the prior state if it fails.`]],
        exam: `Expect the board to want impact analysis, test evidence, a back-out plan, and a schedule. "No clinical impact" is a claim to check, not a reason to approve.` },
      { h: `What you can't trade quietly`,
        body: [`A project holds scope, schedule, budget, and quality together, and moving one moves another. Quality is the one people cut without saying so, by cutting tests. When the date is at risk, make the trade in the open: defer scope through change control, add people or money, or move the date, and let the sponsor decide with the facts.`,
          `Every project draws on the same people, so run them as a portfolio. List every project with its people, rank them against strategy and risk, and defer what can wait, with dates. Then look at the people themselves. Staff competency means a defined skill, practice, and a sign-off from someone who watched, so no skill lives in one person.`],
        terms: [[`Portfolio management`,`Managing all projects together, so shared people and money go to the highest priorities.`],[`Competency validation`,`Confirming by observation or test that a person can perform a defined skill to standard.`]],
        exam: `When the date is threatened, pick the answer that makes the tradeoff visible and keeps the testing. When one person holds a skill, train and verify a second.` },
      { h: `The tickets know first`,
        body: [`Help desk tickets are monitoring data. Group them by time, place, and the words people use, and line them up against recent changes. Twenty tickets that start an hour after a change are one problem, not twenty incidents. Restore service first, then find the root cause.`,
          `Test the rest the same way. Under volume, check the network: clinical traffic gets priority, and scheduled jobs shouldn't all fire in the same minute. In UAT, watch whether each screen puts what the clinician needs where the decision happens. And govern every alert that goes live at a new site, the same as at home.`,
          `Ochoa tore the page off. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"A test that passes tells you less than one that fails,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Love the ones that fail. They're the cheap ones."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Problem management`,`Finding and fixing the root cause behind a pattern of incidents, after service is restored.`],[`Quality of service`,`Network settings that give time-critical traffic priority when a link is busy.`]],
        exam: `For a spike in tickets, correlate before you fix. For a network that slows under load, prioritize clinical traffic and spread scheduled jobs before you buy bandwidth. Alerts at a new site go through the same governance as the rest.` }
    ],
    video: [`sGwm4p9sGPI`,`Software Testing Process Guide: What is User Acceptance Testing, UAT?`,`Online PM Courses, Mike Clayton`,`8:03`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f9s1`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SD1`, title:`Page two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marcus came up from the basement at 7:20 with one of his junior analysts and a printout he'd marked in red. He didn't sit. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Each half works,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"That's the problem."</span>`,
        `It took him four minutes. Tributary turned MedaLink's transfer summary into FHIR and handed the medication list to Corvane twenty at a time, in pages, with a link to the next page at the bottom. Corvane read page one and never asked for page two. The banner Maria had written into script one compared the count to what had arrived, and it took the count from page one, which said twenty. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Tributary tested with a patient on nine meds,"</span> Marcus said. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Corvane tested the banner with a message somebody typed. Both passed. Nobody tested the handoff with more than twenty."</span>`,
        `In her head Maria was back at Irene's kitchen table in May, the two of them saying it to each other. There was a page two. She put her hand flat on the desk and left it there until the room was this year again. Kiran was watching her. So was Marcus. He'd known her long enough to see it, and not long enough to ask.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Kiran set Corvane to read every page tonight, then rerun script one.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`Kiran has Corvane reading every page by ten. Script one passes at noon. Nobody writes the defect down, so nobody checks what else reads FHIR in pages, and two other feeds do.`,
          why:`A quick fix to one symptom isn't a test result. Log the defect, find the cause across both systems, and test everywhere it could live. A same-day fix in the build also skips the change control the build depends on.`},
        {t:`Log a defect with each vendor, and retest when each says it's fixed.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Both vendors fix their halves by Friday. Tributary's fix assumes Corvane will follow the link, and Corvane's assumes Tributary will send one page. Script one fails again, a different way.`,
          why:`Logging it is right. A defect that lives in the handoff needs one owner for the whole path, or each side fixes its own half and the gap moves.`},
        {t:`Log it as severity one, trace the whole handoff, and test the limits.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:6},
          after:`Maria logs it herself, severity one, with the steps, the test patient, twenty-three expected, twenty actual. Marcus owns the path end to end, with both vendors on one call by nine. Corvane will follow every next link, and the banner will take its count from MedaLink's own total. Marcus's analyst writes new scripts at the edges: zero, nineteen, twenty, twenty-one, and forty. She finds two more feeds that read in pages. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Good,"</span> Marcus says, and nothing else.`,
          why:`Integrated testing finds defects that unit tests can't, because they live between systems. Log each one with steps, expected and actual results, and a severity. Give the whole path one owner. Then test the boundaries, where limits like twenty hide.`},
        {t:`Call it a known issue, and have nurses call the sender for long lists.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`It's last winter's workaround, made permanent. And the receiving nurse still can't tell which lists are long, because the screen says twenty.`,
          why:`Accepting a known defect that drops medications at a transfer leaves the safety gap open. A workaround is for the days before a fix, not instead of one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s2`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SD1`, title:`Monday morning`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`That night she called Irene. They talked most Sundays now, but this couldn't wait for Sunday. Maria told it plainly. A test patient, twenty-three medications, twenty on the screen. Irene was quiet a moment. <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Quiet, gentle, direct.">"Where'd you go, when you saw it?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, honest.">"Your kitchen. Then the unit."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Quiet, gentle.">"Me too, just now."</span> Then, drier: <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Dry, warm, steady.">"So you found page two in a test. With nobody in the bed. That's a mock code, Santos."</span>`,
        `Friday, August 6, Corvane's project director sent the plan for the rest of testing. Two more integrated cycles, on August 23 and September 13. Then go-live. Beside stress testing he'd written not needed, since eight hospitals already ran on the same Corvane system every day. User acceptance would be done by Corvane's trainers, who knew the scripts best.`,
        `Gail read it on the conference line. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Your trainers know the scripts,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"My nurses know the nights."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the plan, since the system carries eight hospitals every day already.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The plan is efficient. Nobody tests what happens when three more hospitals sign in on the same Monday morning, and nobody who'll chart in Rawlins touches the build before it's theirs.`,
          why:`Existing load isn't proof of new load. And acceptance by the vendor's trainers isn't acceptance. The people who'll use the system decide whether it's ready.`},
        {t:`Add a volume test at go-live load, UAT by Wyoming staff, and exit criteria.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`The plan comes back Monday with three changes. A volume test on Saturday, September 11, with scripts playing a Monday at seven in the morning: nine hundred users signing in at three hospitals, with orders, results, and transfers all flowing. UAT round one on August 17 and 18, run by Wyoming nurses, pharmacists, and doctors on their own scenarios, Walt included, and a short round two on the final build after build three. And exit criteria, written before anybody knows the score: 95 percent of scripts passed, no open severity one or two defects, every contract acceptance test passed, and a signature from each CNO. Gail reads the last line. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Mine?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Yours."</span>`,
          why:`Each kind of test answers its own question. Volume testing finds where a system slows under real load. User acceptance testing lets the people who'll use it decide whether it's ready. Exit criteria, set in advance, keep anyone from deciding the bar after they've seen the score.`},
        {t:`Add UAT run by Wyoming's nurses and doctors, and trust the load.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Gail's nurses get their two days, and they find real things. Nobody pushes a Monday morning through the system until the first real one.`,
          why:`Putting acceptance in the users' hands is right. A volume test is the only way to find where the system slows before it's carrying patients.`},
        {t:`Swap cycle three for a pilot on one Wyoming unit the week of go-live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Corvane's director likes it for about an hour. A pilot on a live unit is a test with a patient in the bed.`,
          why:`A pilot during go-live doesn't replace testing before it. Find the defects while nobody depends on the system.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s3`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SD3`, title:`Seven point three`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, August 11, the Laramie surgeons' lounge, 7:00 AM. Walt had a clicker in one hand and a copy of the contract open to the acceptance tests. Corvane's trainer sat at the keyboard with a test patient, a routine gallbladder, ready to go home.`,
        `Walt clicked once for every click. He didn't say the number until the end. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Forty-four."</span>`,
        `Corvane's project director was on the phone from Denver. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Forty-four is very close, and three of those are the pending results screen your own council asked for. We'd like to mark 7.3 passed with an exception and sign the build milestone Friday. We'll keep working the clicks after go-live."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Sign it with the exception, since it's close and safety added some clicks.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The milestone is paid Friday. After go-live, keep working the clicks becomes a line in a status report, then a line in a different one.`,
          why:`An acceptance test that fails is a failure, however close. Sign it anyway and you give up the leverage the contract gave you.`},
        {t:`Fail 7.3, log the defect, and hold the milestone until it passes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:3},
          after:`Maria marks it failed in the test record, with Walt's count and the screen where each click happened. The milestone payment waits, as the contract says it does. Corvane's analyst sits with Walt that afternoon, clicker on the table. The pending results screen stays. Two acknowledgments become one signed list, an old diagnosis prompt goes, and the discharge order opens filled in. Friday at seven Walt clicks again. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Thirty-nine."</span> He puts the clicker in his pocket. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Seven point three. Passed. Write it down."</span>`,
          why:`Validate the build against the contract and the design specs, with the acceptance tests written from them. A failed test is logged as a defect, and the deliverable isn't accepted until it passes. The safety steps stay. The waste around them goes.`},
        {t:`Change the requirement to forty-four, since the signed design added steps.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-3,health:0},
          after:`The requirement changes, and the test passes. Walt reads the change notice and asks, in writing, who moved the finish line.`,
          why:`Rewriting a requirement to fit the build turns validation upside down. If the design truly conflicts with the contract, settle it through change control, with the owner of the requirement in the room.`},
        {t:`Accept it with a written exception, and a fix date after go-live.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It's honest, and the date is real. It also pays for a deliverable that doesn't meet the contract, and after go-live Corvane's analysts are busy with everyone else's problems.`,
          why:`Writing down an exception is better than ignoring one. But the contract ties payment to passing, and the fix is easiest before go-live. Hold acceptance until it passes.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s4`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SC2`, title:`Shared parts`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, August 12, the change advisory board met at two, eleven people around the table and Nate on the speaker. Item four was Corvane's. Before go-live, the Wyoming build had to be in Cascade's one Corvane production system, the same system the other eight hospitals used every hour of every day. Corvane asked for standing approval to move it every Thursday night: everything changed in test that week, hidden from the eight hospitals until October.`,
        `The request was two paragraphs long. Risk, low. Clinical impact, none.`,
        `Maria had read those words before, on a firewall change, ten months ago. So had Nate. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"None,"</span> he said over the speaker. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"That's a big word for a Thursday."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Approve it as a standard change, since the build stays hidden till go-live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-4,health:-3},
          after:`It goes through every Thursday with nobody looking. Hidden turns out to mean hidden from users, not from the parts of the system everyone shares.`,
          why:`A standard change is routine work with a known, low risk. Moving a week of untested build into a shared production system is neither. Each move needs a real review.`},
        {t:`Approve the weekly moves, if Corvane tests each package before it moves.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Corvane tests each package. Nobody lists what's in one, or checks it against the eight hospitals already running on the shared parts.`,
          why:`Test evidence is one piece of a change review. The board also needs to know exactly what's moving, what else it touches, and how to undo it.`},
        {t:`Approve each move with a listed manifest, impact check, and back-out plan.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`The board approves it with conditions. Every package lists each change by its ticket. Anything that touches shared content, like the order sets, alerts, and dose times all eleven hospitals use, gets its own ticket and its owner's signature. After each move, a short regression set runs at Greeley and Fort Collins. The back-out plan gets tested once, on purpose, before the first real move. Nate unmutes. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"Better."</span>`,
          why:`Technical change management asks what's changing, what it touches, what proves it's safe, and how to undo it. In a shared system, a change built for three hospitals can reach all eleven, so the impact check covers them all.`},
        {t:`Hold all the Wyoming build out of production until the final week.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`Production stays quiet until October 9. Then the entire build moves in one weekend, untested where it will run, with no time to back anything out.`,
          why:`Saving every change for one late move makes the biggest change the riskiest. Move in controlled steps you can review and undo.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C4`, title:`I don't hover`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, August 17, UAT round one came to Rawlins. Gail had put eight of her nurses on the schedule, nights and days, and paid them to be there. The fixed build passed script one in four minutes. All twenty-three medications arrived inside the admission reconciliation, each labeled with the sending hospital, as the contract said.`,
        `A night nurse with twenty-two years at Rawlins ran the next one: a test patient coming back from Greeley after surgery. She stopped on line nine. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Warfarin,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Did they stop it for surgery, or is it still going? Where's the last dose?"</span>`,
        `The answer was on the screen, under a small gray icon, in text that appeared when you held the mouse over it. Corvane's trainer showed her. The nurse looked at him. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"At three in the morning,"</span> she said, <span class="said" data-who="woman">"I don't hover."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Show each outside med's status and last dose time in its row.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Maria writes it up as a defect against the design, severity two, with the nurse's words in the description. Corvane moves status and last dose into the row itself: active, held, or stopped, with the date and time. Stopped medications stay on the list with a line through them. Pharmacy signs the change. On Wednesday the nurse runs it again and finds the warfarin, stopped two days before surgery, in under three seconds. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Okay,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"That's her yes,"</span> Gail says. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Write it down before she changes her mind."</span>`,
          why:`Functionality helps clinicians when the information for a decision sits where the decision is made, in plain view. Status and last dose time are what a nurse needs to continue or stop an outside medication. UAT is where you learn whether the screen does that for the people who'll use it.`},
        {t:`Pass the script, since every step finished and the data was on the screen.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Script passed. In October, a covering nurse continues a medication Rawlins stopped the day before, because the stop was under an icon.`,
          why:`A script can pass every step and still fail the clinician. UAT asks whether the people who'll use it can make the right decision quickly, and here they couldn't.`},
        {t:`Add a training tip about hovering on the icon, and pass the script.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The tip goes into the training guide on page forty. Most nurses learn it. The ones who float in from another hospital don't.`,
          why:`Training helps, but it asks every nurse to remember a workaround for a screen that hides what they need. Fix the screen.`},
        {t:`Hide stopped outside meds from the list, so nobody can continue them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`Nobody continues a stopped warfarin. Nobody knows it was stopped, either, or why, and the surgeon's plan to restart it on day three is gone from the picture.`,
          why:`Hiding information to prevent one error creates another. A stopped medication is part of the story the nurse needs. Show it, clearly marked.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s6`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SC6`, title:`6:05 AM`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, August 20, Maria was brushing her teeth when the chat came in.`,
        `<prop type="chat" from="Tasha Greene" time="6:05 AM">Pattern. 23 tickets since 9 last night, from Greeley, Fort Collins, and two of the other Corvane hospitals. Most say the same thing. Evening meds showing overdue. BID times look wrong. None from Wyoming. None before last night. Last night was the Thursday build move. I don't think that's a coincidence.</prop>`,
        `Tasha had been right about every early warning she'd sent in a year, and she never sent a second message until somebody answered the first. Maria answered it with the toothbrush still in her mouth.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have the desk work each ticket as it comes in, and watch for more.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The desk resolves them one at a time, kindly. By noon there are fifty-one, each closed as resolved, and the cause is still in production.`,
          why:`Working tickets one by one treats a pattern as separate incidents. When many tickets share a time and a symptom, look for the one change behind them.`},
        {t:`Back out the whole Thursday package, first thing this morning.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`The tickets stop by nine. So does every tested fix the Wyoming build needed, and next Thursday's move has to carry them all again.`,
          why:`Backing out is a fair way to restore service, and that's what the plan is for. Once you know the cause, a targeted back-out protects the rest of the work.`},
        {t:`Match the tickets to the move, undo the cause, then dig.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`Tasha sorts them by the minute. The first came in at 9:12 PM, forty minutes after the move closed. Nineteen of the twenty-three say BID. Nate's team pulls the move's change log, and there it is: the shared frequency table, with BID's evening dose moved from five o'clock to nine for all eleven hospitals. It isn't on the manifest. An emergency change puts it back by 7:20. Pharmacy reviews every evening BID dose given late overnight, forty-one of them, mostly stool softeners and vitamins, and finds no harm. Tasha sends one more line. <span class="said" data-who="Tasha" data-tone="Flat, quiet, sorry.">"Change log says Wednesday, 2:07 AM. Kiran's login. Tell him I'm sorry I'm the one who found it."</span>`,
          why:`Help desk logs are early warning. Group tickets by time, place, and symptom, line them up against recent changes, and treat a cluster as one problem. Restore service first, with the narrowest safe back-out. Then find the root cause.`},
        {t:`Tell nurses to give evening BID doses at the old times for now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The note reaches some units by noon. The eMAR still says nine, so every dose given at five is charted as early, and the overdue alerts keep firing on top of it.`,
          why:`Asking nurses to work around a system error moves the risk to the bedside. Fix the system, and use notes only to bridge the minutes until you do.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s7`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SD2`, title:`212`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Kiran was in her office before she sent for him, gray in the face, with a printout. He'd made the change himself, early Wednesday morning. Cheyenne's nurses gave BID doses at nine and nine, and the Wyoming test patients kept failing on it. He'd meant to change Wyoming's schedule. He'd changed the table all eleven hospitals shared.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Flat, quiet, ashamed.">"I tested it,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Flat, quiet, ashamed.">"Two scripts. Both passed. I was going to file the ticket in the morning, and then the morning was a meeting about something else."</span>`,
        `Corvane's migration tool moved everything changed in test since the last Thursday, ticket or not. The manifest the board had approved listed 211 changes. The tool had moved 212.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take away Kiran's access to the shared tables until after go-live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-5,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`It's quick, and it reads as a sentence. The next shared change gets made by someone with less experience, at the same hour, the same way.`,
          why:`Taking access from one person treats a control failure as a personal one. The same tool and the same process will let the next person do it. Fix the controls.`},
        {t:`Have Kiran check every weekly package against the manifest himself.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`He does it every Thursday at midnight, carefully. It adds four hours to a week that already had none, and the tool still moves by date.`,
          why:`Making the person at the center of the failure into the control adds load and keeps one person doing too many jobs. A control has to live in the process.`},
        {t:`Have a second analyst review Kiran's changes before each weekly move.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Somebody looks over Kiran's work every week. Nobody looks over anyone else's, and the tool still moves anything with a recent date.`,
          why:`A second set of eyes is the right idea. It has to apply to every builder, and the tool has to move only what's been approved.`},
        {t:`Move only ticketed, versioned changes someone else has tested.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`By Tuesday the tool moves by ticket, not by date, and a nightly report compares what's in test with the approved list. Every build gets a version number, and every test result points to one. Nobody tests, approves, or moves their own change. Shared content needs its owner's sign-off: pharmacy for dose times, the council for order sets and alerts. Nate's team reads the audit log every Friday. Kiran reads the new rules. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"It was me."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It was you, and a tool that let one person do four jobs at two in the morning. We're fixing the tool. Go home at five."</span> He nods. He doesn't.`,
          why:`Internal controls during testing keep what was tested and what gets promoted the same thing. Version every build. Move only approved, ticketed changes. Separate the duties, so a builder doesn't test, approve, or promote alone. Then review the audit logs.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s8`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L22`, title:`Two in the morning`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, at 2:10 AM, her phone lit the bedroom ceiling.`,
        `<prop type="chat" from="Kiran Bhatt" time="2:10 AM">BID fixed the right way this time, on Wyoming's schedule, not the shared table. Reran all forty med scripts. All pass. Notes attached. You can tell Ochoa on Tuesday it's handled. Sorry again. It won't happen again.</prop>`,
        `She was in jeans before she'd decided anything. She was on College Avenue with the heater running before it hit her that she was doing exactly what he was doing, at exactly the same hour, for exactly the same reason. She kept driving anyway.`,
        `The analysts' room was dark except for his two monitors. He hadn't eaten. His hands weren't steady. He looked up at her and almost laughed. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Wry, exhausted, a little giddy.">"You drove in at two-forty to tell me not to come in at two."</span>`,
        (G.solofix
          ?`Then he stopped laughing. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, raw, tired.">"Nobody called,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, raw, tired.">"I just kept answering. You restarted Relay yourself at midnight, your first week. Everybody knows that story. I figured that was the job."</span>`
          :`Then he stopped laughing. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, raw, tired.">"Nobody called,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, raw, tired.">"I just kept answering."</span>`),
        `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, honest, heavy.">"You told me in June you were the one they called at two in the morning,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, honest, heavy.">"I heard it. I told you to go home, and then I used every page you made after midnight. The transfer match. The forecast. That whiteboard."</span> She sat down on the next desk. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, heavy, owning it.">"I think I taught you it was a compliment."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Stay and help him finish the open defects, since go-live is close.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`They work until five. They fix three defects and make one new one, which Corvane finds Tuesday. Kiran tells the other analysts the VP came in at two to help, and by September two of them are staying late too.`,
          why:`Joining the overwork teaches the team that it's the job. A leader who models two in the morning gets a team that works at two in the morning, and makes the mistakes people make then.`},
        {t:`Take him home, route his fix through change control, train a backup.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:7,conf:4,health:6},
          after:`She drives him home herself and leaves his car in the garage. He's asleep before the second light. His fix waits for Monday, a ticket, and somebody else's test, though she can see it's probably right. She calls him Sunday at noon and tells him he's off with pay through Friday, and she doesn't let him argue. On Monday she writes his job down the way she wrote Marcus's in December: lead medication analyst, responsible for building the skill in others. One of the junior analysts Marcus has been teaching starts building medications beside Kiran, then while he watches, then alone, with a sign-off for each skill. It costs Maria her best builder for a week, eight weeks from go-live, and she tells Ochoa so on Tuesday, in those words. At the top of Monday's page she writes KIRAN, as always. Under it, smaller, in her own hand: and me.`,
          why:`Staff competency can't rest on one person working all night. Define the skill, train a second person, watch them do it, and sign it off. Care for the person first. Then fix the role, so the system doesn't need a hero at two in the morning.`},
        {t:`Thank him, send him home, and let his fix move Thursday, since it passed.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He goes home, which helps. His fix moves Thursday with nobody else having tested it, which is the same hole, with a ticket on it this time.`,
          why:`Caring for him is right. His fix still needs someone else to test it, and the deeper problem, that only he can build medications, is still there on Monday.`},
        {t:`Write him up for Wednesday's unlogged change, so the rule sticks.`, s:0, d:{trust:-6,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`The rule sticks. So does the lesson the team takes from it: report less. Kiran stops telling her what he works on at night. He doesn't stop working on it.`,
          why:`Disciplining someone for a failure the process allowed teaches people to hide problems. Fix the controls, care for the person, and build the skill in more than one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s9`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SC4`, title:`The receiver`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, August 23, build two ran: 140 scripts, the first 120 plus twenty new ones at the edges. 126 passed. Fourteen failed. The exit criteria said 95 percent.`,
        `Tuesday at 7:30, Ochoa had an email from Corvane's project director on his desk before Maria sat down. To hold October 16, Corvane proposed that build three rerun only the fourteen failed scripts, not all 140. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Full regression is a luxury at this stage,"</span> the email said. It was addressed to Ochoa, with Maria copied.`,
        `Ochoa read it. Then he picked up his cell, found Corvane's vice president in the contacts, and his thumb stayed over the name. Maria watched him. He set the phone face down on the table, slowly, the way you set down something hot.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Dry, quiet, a little rueful.">"I was about to call him and fix this for you,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Dry, quiet, a little rueful.">"Old habit. Thirty years of it."</span> He slid the email across the table and turned his legal pad face down. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"It's your project. Tell me what you decide."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rerun only the fourteen failed scripts in build three, to hold the date.`, s:0, flag:`skippedregression`, d:{trust:-3,conf:-4,health:-4},
          after:`Build three will run fourteen scripts. Nobody will rerun the other 126, which have had a month of fixes made around them since they last passed. The date holds. So will whatever those fixes broke.`,
          why:`Skipping regression trades quality for schedule without saying so. Every fix can break something that passed. Rerun the full set, and make any tradeoff in the open.`},
        {t:`Keep full regression, defer two low-risk items, and add the vendor's testers.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Maria answers it in one page. Full regression stays. Two items come out of go-live through change control, each with its owner's agreement: video visits in the Wyoming portal, which nobody needs on day one, and a custom surgery report Laramie can pull from the warehouse until January. Corvane adds four testers for six weeks at its own cost, under the clause that ties its payments to acceptance. Go or no-go is set for Friday, October 8, against the criteria everyone signed in August. Ochoa reads the page, initials the bottom, and doesn't touch the phone again.`,
          why:`Scope, schedule, budget, and quality move together. When the date is at risk, defer scope through change control, add resources, or move the date, in the open, with the sponsor. Don't cut testing quietly. Quality is the constraint nobody sees until go-live.`},
        {t:`Move go-live back a month, so every test can still run at full scope.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's safe, and it's early. The exit criteria haven't been missed yet, and a month's delay means a second round of training for every Wyoming nurse and a holiday cutover.`,
          why:`Moving the date is a real option, and sometimes the right one. Decide it against the exit criteria, once you can see them missed, after you've looked at scope and resources.`},
        {t:`Have the analysts test nights and weekends until build three passes.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`They do. Defects found at midnight get fixed at one by the same tired people, and the pass rate climbs while the error rate in the fixes climbs right along with it.`,
          why:`Overtime as the plan burns out the team and lowers the quality you're testing for. Change scope, resources, or the date, and do it openly.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s10`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L23`, title:`Five projects`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, August 25, with Kiran home for the week, Maria pulled up everything with his name on it. One Chart's medication build. The order set reviews for the council. The FHIR feed spec for Aisha's Meadowlark patch pilot, due September 15. The chart views for Bev's remote ICU pilot, due October 1. And the mapping for Penwright's discharge instructions, phase two, set to start the month after go-live.`,
        `Five projects. Each one had an owner who'd been told yes. Aisha's email came in while Maria was still counting. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Hope Kiran's okay. Is the Meadowlark spec still on for the fifteenth? The cardiologists are excited."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep every date, and split Kiran's projects among the other analysts.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Eight analysts absorb a ninth one's work in September, the month before go-live. Two of them start coming in early. One starts coming in at five.`,
          why:`Spreading one overloaded person's work across a team that's already full doesn't add capacity. It moves the overload. Decide what waits.`},
        {t:`Bring in two contract analysts to cover his projects until go-live.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The contractors are good, and they need three weeks to learn Cascade's build. Kiran spends his first week back training them.`,
          why:`Adding people can help, but new people cost time before they save it. Rank the work first, then decide where help goes.`},
        {t:`Rank every project and its people through governance, and defer.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`By Friday it's one page. Fourteen active projects, nine analysts, the team booked at 131 percent for September. The IT steering committee ranks them by strategy and risk, with Ochoa, Glenn's analyst, and Aisha in the room. One Chart comes first, and the fall security patches for the eight Corvane hospitals stay, because they can't wait. The Meadowlark spec moves to November 15, with the pilot still on for February. Phase two mapping moves a month. Bev's chart views stay, because they're small and her pilot needs them, and a junior analyst takes them. Kiran's five projects become two. Aisha writes back. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Fine. November fifteenth, in writing."</span> Maria calls Theo about phase two herself. It's the call she least wants to make. He hears her out. <span class="said" data-who="Theo" data-tone="Easy, plain, a little rueful.">"A month,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Theo" data-tone="Easy, plain, a little rueful.">"My boss will yell, and I'll tell him it's right, because it is."</span> Then: <span class="said" data-who="Theo" data-tone="Warm, teasing.">"Irene says you call her Sundays now."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"She told you?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Theo" data-tone="Warm, teasing.">"She tells everybody."</span>`,
          why:`Projects share the same people, so manage them as a portfolio. Put every project and its staffing on one page, rank them against strategy and risk through governance, and defer what can wait, each with a new date its owner knows.`},
        {t:`Pause everything but One Chart until go-live, so the team can focus.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The team focuses. The fall security patches for the eight Corvane hospitals wait too, and Nate finds out from the pause notice.`,
          why:`A blanket pause skips the ranking. Some work can't wait, like security patches. Rank every project, then pause the ones that can.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s11`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E7`, title:`Seven in the morning`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, September 11, the volume test played a Monday morning that hadn't happened yet. Scripts signed in nine hundred users at Cheyenne, Laramie, and Rawlins, placed orders, scanned doses, printed labels, and sent transfers through Tributary, at the rate the census numbers said a real Monday would.`,
        `At 7:15 on the test clock, Rawlins slowed. Charts took nine seconds to open. Marcus had the network graphs up beside the queues. Every fifteen minutes, the Rawlins circuit jumped to 96 percent for about forty seconds, then dropped back.`,
        `Nate found it first. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"Your downtime computers,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"Every one of them refreshes its copy in the same minute. And the imaging system pulls yesterday's studies right then too."</span> They'd built the downtime view to protect Rawlins. At the top of every quarter hour, it was choking it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Turn off the downtime view refresh at Rawlins until after go-live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Charts open fast. The downtime computers hold a census from September for a month, which is the one thing they were built not to do.`,
          why:`Turning off a safety control to fix performance trades one risk for a worse one. Find a way to run both.`},
        {t:`Accept the slowdown, since it lasts forty seconds four times an hour.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Forty seconds, four times an hour, is the whole morning med pass in pieces. Nurses learn to wait for it, and then learn to work around it.`,
          why:`Slowness at peak load is exactly what a volume test exists to find. Fix it before it costs clinicians their time.`},
        {t:`Order a bigger circuit for Rawlins, and rerun the test when it's in.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The carrier quotes ninety days. More bandwidth would help, and it arrives in January.`,
          why:`More capacity is a fair long-term answer. The faster fix is in how the traffic is scheduled and prioritized, and you can test it this week.`},
        {t:`Stagger the refreshes, give clinical traffic priority, and rerun it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`Marcus spreads the refreshes across the fifteen minutes, one unit at a time. Nate's team sets quality of service on the Rawlins routers, so the chart and the Tributary feeds go first, ahead of imaging and the guest Wi-Fi. The imaging pull moves to two in the morning. They run the whole test again the next Saturday. Rawlins charts open in under two seconds, and the circuit tops out at 61 percent. Gail, on the video call from Rawlins, watches the graph flatten. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"So the fix was to stop doing everything at once,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Usually is."</span>`,
          why:`Infrastructure has to carry clinical work at peak load. Test it under volume, find what competes for the network, and schedule and prioritize traffic so the clinical record goes first. Buy bandwidth when that isn't enough.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s12`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C6`, title:`Four hundred twelve`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, September 14, the Clinical Informatics Council looked at the last list before go-live. Corvane's build team had loaded every alert in Corvane's standard content into the Wyoming build: 412 of them. Cascade's own governed library, the one the council had spent the spring cleaning, had 231, each with an owner and a review date.`,
        (G.allalerts
          ?`It was worse than that. The five alerts Aisha had turned on with the sepsis pilot in February, with no owners, were on Corvane's list too. Greeley's director of nursing had asked twice since then to have them taken off her units. Now they were headed for Wyoming.`
          :`Aisha had the two lists side by side. A hundred and eighty-one alerts were on Corvane's list and not on hers.`),
        `Gail was on the speaker from Rawlins. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"My nurses have lived with MedaLink's six alerts for nine years,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"How many do they get on October sixteenth?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Go live with all 412, and review the whole list after ninety days.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-4,health:-4},
          after:`Ninety days of 412 alerts teaches Wyoming's nurses what it taught Greeley's: click through. The review in January starts by undoing it.`,
          why:`Vendor default content isn't governed content. Go live with what your council has approved, owned, and measured, and add the rest only through intake.`},
        {t:`Go live with Cascade's 231 governed alerts, and add more on request.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's the right list. Nobody checks which of the 231 fit a 22-bed hospital with swing beds, or watches how Wyoming's nurses answer them, until the requests start.`,
          why:`Governed content is right. A new site still needs its own clinicians to review fit, and override monitoring from the first day to catch what doesn't fit.`},
        {t:`Use the governed 231, reviewed by Wyoming, and track every override.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`The council keeps Cascade's 231 and drops the rest. The three Wyoming chairs go through the 231 in two sessions and flag nine that don't fit, like a fall reassessment timed for a night tech Rawlins doesn't have. Those get Wyoming versions, each with an owner. Sepsis version two goes live with both tiers, the board column and the tight pop-up. One of Marcus's analysts builds a dashboard of override rates by alert, unit, and hospital, read every week for the first ninety days. Gail gets her answer: sixty-three alerts that interrupt, and a name to call about each one.`,
          why:`Decision support needs ongoing governance at every site: approved content with owners and review dates, a review by local clinicians for fit, and override monitoring from the first day, so the content keeps getting better.`},
        {t:`Turn off every interruptive alert for the first month, to keep it calm.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`Go-live is calm. Without the allergy and dose range alerts, a Laramie resident orders a full adult dose for a ninety-pound woman, and pharmacy catches it at 3 AM.`,
          why:`Some interruptive alerts are essential safety checks. Govern the list. Don't switch off the alerts that protect patients.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f9s9`, title:`Pass rate and defect density by build`, setup:[`Every build gets the same two numbers. Pass rate = scripts passed / scripts executed. Defect density = defects logged / scripts executed. Cascade counts density per test script, not per function point, and uses the same measure for every build.`,`Build 1 ran on August 2: 120 scripts executed, 89 passed, 48 defects logged. Some failed scripts found more than one defect. Build 2 ran on August 23: 140 scripts executed, the first 120 plus 20 new ones at the edges. 126 passed, and 21 defects were logged.`,`The exit criteria call for at least 95 percent of all 140 scripts to pass in build 3. Round percents to two decimals and density to two decimals.`],
      table:[[`Build`,`Run`,`Passed`,`Defects`],[`1`,`120`,`89`,`48`],[`2`,`140`,`126`,`21`]],
      fields:[{label:`Build 1 pass rate, percent`, answer:74.17, tol:0.01},{label:`Build 1 defects per script`, answer:0.4, tol:0.005},{label:`Build 2 pass rate, percent`, answer:90, tol:0.01},{label:`Build 2 defects per script`, answer:0.15, tol:0.005},{label:`Drop in defect density, build 1 to 2, percent`, answer:62.5, tol:0.01},{label:`Scripts build 3 must pass to meet 95 percent`, answer:133, tol:0}],
      solution:[`Build 1 pass rate: 89 / 120 = 0.74167, or 74.17 percent. Thirty-one failed: 120 minus 89.`,`Build 1 density: 48 / 120 = 0.40 defects per script.`,`Build 2 pass rate: 126 / 140 = 0.90, or 90.00 percent. Fourteen failed: 140 minus 126.`,`Build 2 density: 21 / 140 = 0.15 defects per script.`,`Drop in density: (0.40 minus 0.15) / 0.40 = 0.25 / 0.40 = 0.625, or 62.50 percent.`,`Exit criteria: 0.95 x 140 = 133 scripts. Build 2 passed 126, so build 3 needs 7 more passes: 133 minus 126. It can fail no more than 7 of the 140.`,`Both numbers improved, and build 2 still misses the criteria. That's why the fourteen failures can't be the only scripts in build 3. Every fix since August 2 could have broken one of the 126 that passed.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f9s2`, title:`Name the test type`, intro:`Every test answers a different question. Pick the type each one is.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Tributary's engineer checks that her mapping turns one MedaLink medication into one FHIR resource, with no other system involved.`, choices:[`Unit`,`System`,`Integrated`,`Regression`,`Stress or volume`,`User acceptance`], answer:0, why:`One component tested by itself, usually by its builder, is unit testing.`},
      {prompt:`Corvane's team checks every screen, rule, and order set in the Wyoming build inside Corvane alone, against the design.`, choices:[`Unit`,`System`,`Integrated`,`Regression`,`Stress or volume`,`User acceptance`], answer:1, why:`System testing checks one whole application against its design, before it's tested with the systems around it.`},
      {prompt:`A test patient is registered at Rawlins, given medications, and transferred to Greeley, across MedaLink, Tributary, and Corvane.`, choices:[`Unit`,`System`,`Integrated`,`Regression`,`Stress or volume`,`User acceptance`], answer:2, why:`A workflow that crosses systems and interfaces end to end is integrated testing. That's where the page two defect lived.`},
      {prompt:`After the paging defect is fixed, the team reruns the 89 scripts that had already passed.`, choices:[`Unit`,`System`,`Integrated`,`Regression`,`Stress or volume`,`User acceptance`], answer:3, why:`Rerunning passed tests after a change, to prove the fix didn't break them, is regression testing.`},
      {prompt:`Scripts sign in 900 users at 7 AM at three hospitals while orders, results, and transfers flow at Monday's rate.`, choices:[`Unit`,`System`,`Integrated`,`Regression`,`Stress or volume`,`User acceptance`], answer:4, why:`Pushing go-live load through the system to see where it slows or fails is stress or volume testing.`},
      {prompt:`Rawlins night nurses run their own scenarios on the new build and sign whether it's ready for their unit.`, choices:[`Unit`,`System`,`Integrated`,`Regression`,`Stress or volume`,`User acceptance`], answer:5, why:`The people who'll use the system deciding whether it's ready is user acceptance testing.`},
      {prompt:`After a change to the shared dose time table, the team reruns medication scripts at Greeley, a hospital the change wasn't meant for.`, choices:[`Unit`,`System`,`Integrated`,`Regression`,`Stress or volume`,`User acceptance`], answer:3, why:`Checking that a change didn't break something that already worked, even somewhere it wasn't aimed, is regression testing.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`After a defect is fixed, the team reruns test scripts that had already passed. What kind of testing is this?`, opts:[`Unit testing`,`Regression testing`,`Volume testing`,`Acceptance testing`], a:1, why:`Regression testing reruns tests that passed before, after any change, to prove the fix didn't break something else. Unit testing checks one piece alone, volume testing checks peak load, and acceptance testing is the users' decision on readiness.`, task:`SD1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Two vendors each pass their own tests, but a transferred patient's list of 23 medications arrives in the new EHR with 20. Which testing is designed to find this?`, opts:[`Unit testing of each vendor's own piece`,`Stress testing at peak user load`,`Security testing of each interface`,`Integrated testing across systems`], a:3, why:`Integrated testing runs real workflows across connected systems, where defects in the handoff between them show up. Each vendor's unit tests passed because each half worked alone.`, task:`SD1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Who should perform user acceptance testing of a new EHR build?`, opts:[`Trainers from the vendor`,`Analysts on the project`,`Clinicians who'll use it`,`An outside testing firm`], a:2, why:`UAT is done by the people who will use the system, on their own scenarios, and its owners sign off. Vendor trainers and analysts test earlier phases.`, task:`SD1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`In a test cycle, 160 scripts were executed and 136 passed. What is the pass rate?`, opts:[`85 percent`,`15 percent`,`88 percent`,`80 percent`], a:0, why:`Pass rate = scripts passed / scripts executed = 136 / 160 = 0.85, or 85 percent. Fifteen percent is the share that failed: 24 / 160.`, task:`SD1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`An analyst builds a change, tests it himself, and moves it into production on his own. Which internal control is MISSING?`, opts:[`Segregation of duties`,`Encryption of test data`,`A tested recovery plan`,`Two-factor sign-in rules`], a:0, why:`When one person can build, test, and promote a change, nobody independent checks it. Segregation of duties splits those steps among different people.`, task:`SD2`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A contract acceptance test requires 41 clicks or fewer. The build takes 44, and the vendor asks to sign the milestone with an exception. What should the IT leader do?`, opts:[`Sign it, since three clicks are close`,`Hold acceptance until the test passes`,`Rewrite the requirement to forty-four`,`Sign it, with a fix date after go-live`], a:1, why:`Validate deliverables against the contract and design specs. A failed acceptance test is a defect, and the deliverable isn't accepted, or paid for, until it passes as written.`, task:`SD3`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Eleven hospitals share one EHR production system. A vendor asks to move weekly build for three new hospitals into it. What should the change advisory board require?`, opts:[`Standing approval as a standard change`,`Vendor testing of each package first`,`One single move in the final week`,`A manifest, impact check, and back-out`], a:3, why:`A change review needs to know exactly what's moving, what else it touches, the test evidence, and how to undo it. In a shared system, impact reaches every hospital on it.`, task:`SC2`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Three weeks before go-live, the pass rate is below the exit criteria, and the vendor proposes rerunning only the failed scripts. What is the BEST response?`, opts:[`Rerun only failed scripts, to keep the date`,`Have staff test nights until the rate is met`,`Keep full regression; trade scope or staff`,`Move go-live right away by at least a month`], a:2, why:`Skipping regression cuts quality quietly. Make the tradeoff in the open: defer scope through change control, add resources, or move the date if the criteria are missed.`, task:`SC4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Twenty help desk tickets about wrong medication times arrive from four hospitals within hours of a build move. What should the service desk do FIRST?`, opts:[`Correlate all the tickets with the change`,`Resolve each ticket as it's reported`,`Ask the vendor to explain the tickets`,`Send units a workaround for dose times`], a:0, why:`Tickets that share a time and a symptom are one problem. Line them up against recent changes to find the cause, restore service, then fix the root.`, task:`SC6`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Outside medications appear in admission reconciliation, but their status and last dose time show only when a nurse hovers over an icon. What change BEST supports the nurse's decision?`, opts:[`Add a tip on hovering to the training`,`Hide medications the sender stopped`,`Add a pop-up telling nurses to hover`,`Show status and last dose in each row`], a:3, why:`Functionality supports clinical decisions when the needed information is in plain view where the decision is made. Training and pop-ups work around the screen, and hiding stopped medications hides part of the story.`, task:`C4`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A vendor loads all 412 of its standard alerts into the build for three new hospitals. The health system's governed library has 231. What is the BEST approach?`, opts:[`Go live with all 412 and review later`,`Go live with no interruptive alerts`,`Governed set, with a local review`,`Let each hospital pick its own alerts`], a:2, why:`Go live with content the governance process has approved, owned, and measured, have local clinicians review it for fit, and monitor overrides from day one. Vendor defaults aren't governed content.`, task:`C6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Only one analyst can build medication content, and he works nights to keep up. What BEST reduces this risk?`, opts:[`Ask him to document his work in detail`,`Train a second analyst, with sign-offs`,`Hire a contractor to cover his nights`,`Limit his build access to work hours`], a:1, why:`Staff competency comes from a defined skill, supervised practice, and a sign-off by someone who watched. A second trained analyst removes the single point of failure.`, task:`L22`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`An analyst is assigned to five projects, and the team is booked at 131 percent for the month. What should the IT leader do FIRST?`, opts:[`Spread his projects across the team`,`Pause every project except the EHR`,`Hire contractors for each project`,`Rank projects through governance`], a:3, why:`Manage projects as a portfolio: list them with their people, rank them against strategy and risk through governance, and defer what can wait, with dates.`, task:`L23`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Testers can't say which build configuration produced last week's passing results, and a fix has gone in since. Which control is MISSING?`, opts:[`Version control`,`A traceability matrix`,`Quality of service`,`An exit criterion`], a:0, why:`Version control gives every build a number and ties each test result to it, so a pass points to exactly what passed. A traceability matrix links requirements to tests, and exit criteria set the bar, but neither says which build was tested.`, task:`SD2`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A volume test shows a rural hospital's circuit saturating every 15 minutes, when scheduled downtime copies all refresh at once. What is the BEST first fix?`, opts:[`Turn off the downtime copies until go-live`,`Order a circuit with twice the bandwidth`,`Stagger refreshes, put the EHR first`,`Accept it, since each slowdown is brief`], a:2, why:`Spread scheduled jobs so they don't compete, and give clinical traffic priority. Turning off the downtime copies removes a safety control, and new bandwidth takes months.`, task:`E7`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Friday, September 17, build three finished at 4:40 in the afternoon.`,
    ((s.flags.skippedregression||G.skippedregression)
      ?`<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="One Chart test pass rate by build" labels="Build 1,Build 2,Build 3" values="74,90,100">Percent of scripts executed that passed. Build 1, August 2, 120 scripts. Build 2, August 23, 140 scripts. Build 3, September 13 to 17, the fourteen scripts that failed in build 2.</prop>`
      :`<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="One Chart test pass rate by build" labels="Build 1,Build 2,Build 3" values="74,90,98">Percent of scripts executed that passed. Build 1, August 2, 120 scripts. Build 2, August 23, 140 scripts. Build 3, September 13 to 17, all 140 scripts, full regression.</prop>`),
    ((s.flags.skippedregression||G.skippedregression)
      ?`A hundred percent of fourteen. It was the best bar on the chart. The other 126 scripts hadn't run since August 23, and a month of fixes had been made around them since. Maria looked at the third bar for a long time, the way Walt had once looked at a report that put Rawlins at ninety-eight.`
      :`One hundred thirty-seven of 140. No open severity one or two defects. Three severity threes, each with a workaround on paper and a fix on the calendar. Every contract acceptance test passed, 7.3 included. UAT round two ran on that same build on September 20 and 21. Gail signed for Rawlins Tuesday afternoon, the Cheyenne and Laramie CNOs on Wednesday, and Walt, unasked, initialed the surgery page.`),
    `Kiran had come back on Monday, August 30, and gone home at five that day, and most days since. The analyst Marcus had been teaching had her first three medication sign-offs on the analysts' whiteboard, in Kiran's handwriting. On Wednesday the cafeteria cashier stopped Maria at the register. The young man was back, she said. Every day at 12:15. Then she looked at Maria's tray. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Warm, offhand.">"You, too, lately."</span>`,
    `Sunday, September 19, Lucia came home from Greeley for dinner with a bag of laundry and four weeks of nursing school in her voice. They'd practiced medication reconciliation in skills lab with plastic pill bottles, and her instructor made them read every bottle against the list out loud, twice. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"She says you count everything,"</span> Lucia said. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Even when it looks finished."</span> Maria passed the potatoes and didn't say anything at all.`,
    `On her way out to the car, Lucia stopped in the kitchen doorway. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Light, pointed, sure.">"Twenty-seven days."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Till what?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Light, pointed, sure.">"October sixteenth. You said after go-live."</span> She'd been counting. Of course she had. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, warm, sure.">"That's a date,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"That's a date."</span>`,
    `Saturday, September 25, Maria was on the back step with her first coffee when Gail called at 8:14. Gail never called on a weekend.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Gail" data-tone="Low, tight, controlled.">"Our clinic across town opens for walk-ins at eight on Saturdays,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail" data-tone="Low, tight, controlled.">"The front desk turned the computers on. Every screen in the building has the same note on it. Somebody wants money. The manager's reading it to Nate right now."</span>`,
    `Maria was already reaching for her keys. It was three weeks to the day before go-live.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch09.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Lock`
});
